3.text:
In a linear Stokes flow the stream function can be written in the form
$\psi(x,z)=\Phi(z)\sin kx$, $u_{x}=\psi_{z}$, $u_{z}=-\psi_{x}$,
so that with $s=k h$

\begin{equation*}
\nabla^{4}\psi =0\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad 
\Phi^{(iv)}-2k^{2}\Phi''+k^{4}\Phi=0 ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\Phi(z)=\big(a+bz\big)e^{kz}+\big(c+dz\big)e^{-kz}\; .
\end{equation*}
With the kinematic condition $e_{t}=u_{z}(0)$ the steady state is
$\Phi(0)=0$ and with the no–slip condition on the rigid lid
$u_{x}(0)=0$ we have

\begin{equation*}
\Phi(0)=0,\qquad\Phi'(0)=0\;\Longleftrightarrow\;c=-a,\;
d=-b-2ka .
\end{equation*}
On a free surface the tangential stress vanishes, $\sigma_{xz}(0)=0$,
i.e. $\Phi''(0)+k^{2}\Phi(0)=0$ and with $\Phi(0)=0$ we get
$\Phi''(0)=0$.  Hence $b=d$ and

\begin{equation*}
b=d=-ka ,
\qquad 
u_{x}=k a\Big[e^{kz}-e^{-kz}\Big]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(z)=\mu\big[\Phi''+k^{2}\Phi\big]\sin kx
=-4\mu k^{3}az\cosh kz\,\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
The basal traction $\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx$ gives

\begin{equation*}
-4\mu k^{3}ah\cosh s=S_{0},\qquad 
a=-\frac{S_{0}}{4\mu k^{3}h\cosh s},
\end{equation*}
so that with
$p(z)=-\mu k^{-1}\big[\Phi'''-k^{2}\Phi'\big]\cos kx$
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}= \Big[\frac{\mu}{k}\Phi'''-3\mu k\Phi'-p_{0}\Big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Putting $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ and $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e_{s}\cos kx$ the
pressure $p_{0}$ drops out and with $\Phi'(0)=0$, $\Phi(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}=
\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[\Phi'''(0)-\Phi'''(h)\Big]
+3\mu k\Phi'(h) .
\end{equation*}
Using $b=d=-ka$ this becomes

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{2\mu k^{2}a}{\rho g}
\Big[\,-1+(1-s)e^{s}+se^{-s}\Big],
\qquad s=k h,
\end{equation*}
and with the value of $a$ above

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
\frac{\,e^{s}(1-s)+se^{-s}-1\,}{2s\cosh s}
 =\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;
\frac{\,2s\sinh s-e^{s}+1\,}{2s\cosh s}\;,
\end{equation*}
the sign being that of the imposed traction.  In particular
\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac{\,2s\sinh s-e^{s}+1\,}{2s\cosh s}
      =\frac{e^{s}(1-s)+se^{-s}-1}{2s\cosh s},
\end{equation*}
is the dimensionless response of the lid to a basal shear $S_{0}$.
For the long wave, thin plate limit $s=k h\ll1$

\begin{equation*}
\chi(s)=\frac12-\frac34\,s+O(s^{2}),
\end{equation*}
so that to leading order in $k h$

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\frac12\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\;,
\qquad 
\chi_{0}= \frac12 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to the conventional sign $e_{s}= \chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ with
$\chi\to 1/2$ in the usual $k h\ll1$ geodynamic limit.  With the
sign convention $e_{s}>0$ for an upward deflection the magnitude is
$|e_{s}|=| \chi| S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ and the often quoted
long–wave value is $|\chi|\approx 1/2$, which is the $k h\to0$ limit of
the exact expression above.  The $s$–dependent form is normally written
as $e_{s}= \chi(s)S_{0}/\rho g$ with $\chi(s)$ given above; with
$\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is the required stable topographic
amplitude.  The $s\to0$ limit $\chi\to1/2$ is the form usually quoted
in the literature. ∎

1.text:
In a $x,z$ system with $z=0$ at the top of the plate and $z=h$ at the
basal interface put

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=A(z)\sin kx , \qquad u_{z}=B(z)\cos kx ,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx  ,
\end{equation*}
with $k=2\pi /\lambda $.  Incompressibility
$\nabla\!\cdot\!u=0$ gives
\begin{equation*}
B'=-kA ,
\end{equation*}
and the Stokes equations
\begin{equation*}
-\nabla p+\mu\nabla^{2}u=0
\end{equation*}
give with $\sigma_{ij}=-p\delta_{ij}
+\mu\bigl(u_{i,j}+u_{j,i}\bigr)$

\begin{equation*}
P=-\mu B'+\frac{\mu}{k^{2}}B''',\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\Bigl[-\frac{B''}{k}-kB\Bigr]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}B=0 ,\qquad D=\frac{d}{dz} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
B(z)=\bigl(p_{0}+p_{1}z\bigr)\cosh kz+
\bigl(q_{0}+q_{1}z\bigr)\sinh kz .
\end{equation*}
The top conditions
\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad \sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e(t)\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
are
\begin{equation*}
B'(0)=0,\qquad -P(0)=\rho g e ,
\end{equation*}
and with $B'(0)=0$,
\begin{equation*}
P(0)=\frac{\mu}{k^{2}}B'''(0),\qquad 
B'''(0)=-\frac{k^{2}\rho g}{\mu}e .
\end{equation*}
Putting  $B(0)=0$ for the steady kinematic condition $u_{z}(0)=0$ one
may write
\begin{equation*}
B(z)=p\;z\cosh kz+\Bigl(-\frac{p}{k}+D\,z\Bigr)\sinh kz ,
\qquad p_{1}\equiv p ,
\end{equation*}
so that
\[
B'(0)=0,\qquad B'''(0)=2k^{2}p=
-\frac{k^{2}\rho g}{\mu}e,
\]
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
p=-\frac{\rho g}{2\mu}e .
\end{equation*}
The basal conditions
\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx
\end{equation*}
are
\begin{equation*}
P(h)=2\mu B'(h),\qquad 
-\frac{B''(h)}{k}-kB(h)=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu},
\end{equation*}
which with the above form of $B$ give
\begin{equation*}
B'''(h)=3k^{2}B'(h),\qquad 
p\bigl[\cosh\alpha-\alpha\sinh\alpha\bigr]=
\alpha\cosh\alpha\,D  ,
\end{equation*}
$\alpha=k\,h$, and
\begin{equation*}
-2\Bigl[p\,\alpha\cosh\alpha+
D\bigl(\cosh\alpha+\alpha\sinh\alpha\bigr)\Bigr]=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu} .
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $D$,
\begin{equation*}
D=p\Bigl(\frac1\alpha-\tanh\alpha\Bigr),
\end{equation*}
and the shear condition becomes
\begin{equation*}
-2p\,\frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
=\frac{S_{0}}{\mu} .
\end{equation*}
With $p=-\rho ge/2\mu$ this is the steady state
\begin{equation*}
\rho g\,e\,\frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\qquad\alpha=k h .
\end{equation*}
In particular, with the density contrast $\Delta\rho$ which appears in
the free–surface restoring stress $\sigma_{zz}=\Delta\rho\,g\,e\cos kx$
the usual form is written
\[
e_{s}= \chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
       =\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
       \frac{k h\cosh k h}{(k h)^{2}+\cosh^{2}k h},
\]
which for the notation used in the statement $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$
is
\[
\chi(kh)=\frac{k h\cosh k h}{(k h)^{2}+\cosh^{2} k h}
       =\frac{k h}{\cosh k h+\displaystyle\frac{(k h)^{2}}{\cosh k h}} .
\]
In the thin–plate, long–wavelength limit $\alpha=k h\ll1$,
$\cosh\alpha=1+O(\alpha^{2})$ and
\[
\chi(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq\,k h\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
      =\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\]
i.e. to leading order the dynamic topography is linear in the
non–dimensional plate thickness $\alpha=k h$.  For the often quoted
order–of–magnitude value $\alpha\approx1$,
$\chi\approx0.46$, $e_{s}\approx0.46\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, the familiar
$S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ scaling with an $O(1)$ geometrical factor.  The
factor $\rho/\Delta\rho$ is unity if $\rho$ in $\sigma_{zz}=\rho g e$
is identified with the density contrast $\Delta\rho$.  This is the
viscously relaxed, i.e. $t\to\infty$, topography driven by the harmonic
basal traction $S_{0}\sin kx$.

