\begin{equation*}
P=-\mu B'+\frac{\mu}{k^{2}}B''',\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\Big[-\frac{B''}{k}-kB\Big]\sin kx ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\bigl(D^{2}-k^{2}\bigr)^{2}B=0 ,\qquad D=d/dz,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $B(z)=(p_{0}+p_{1}z)\cosh kz+(q_{0}+q_{1}z)\sinh kz$.
For the steady deflected lid

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0,\qquad u_{z}(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that with the material kinematic condition $ \dot e=u_{z}(0)$

\begin{equation*}
B(0)=0,\qquad B'(0)=0 .
\end{equation*}
Writing the biharmonic with $B(0)=B'(0)=0$,

\begin{equation*}
B(z)=p\,z\cosh kz+\Big[-p/k+q_{1}z\Big]\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
B'(z)=q_{1}kz\cosh kz+\bigl(pkz+q_{1}\bigr)\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=\mu k^{-2}B'''(z)-\mu B'(z),\qquad 
P(0)=\frac{\mu}{k^{2}}B'''(0).
\end{equation*}
The top normal stress is $-P(0)=\rho ge$ so that with $B'(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
P(0)=\frac{\mu}{k^{2}}B'''(0),\qquad 
B'''(0)=-\frac{k^{2}\rho g}{\mu}e ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
B'''(0)=2k^{2}p,\qquad 
p=-\frac{\rho g}{2\mu}e .
\end{equation*}
The bottom conditions are

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\;\Longleftrightarrow\;P(h)=2\mu B'(h),\qquad
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
With $P(h)=2\mu B'(h)$

\begin{equation*}
B'''(h)=3k^{2}B'(h)
\end{equation*}
which with the form of $B$ gives

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}=p\Big[\frac1{k h}-\tanh k h\Big],\qquad\alpha\equiv kh .
\end{equation*}
The basal shear is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=\mu\Big[-\frac{B''(h)}{k}-kB(h)\Big]
     =-2\mu\Big[q_{1}\cosh\alpha+q_{1}\alpha\sinh\alpha
           +p\alpha\cosh\alpha\Big]\sin kx .
\end{equation*}
Hence with the $q_{1}$ above

\begin{equation*}
q_{1}\cosh\alpha+q_{1}\alpha\sinh\alpha+p\alpha\cosh\alpha
=p\,\frac{\cosh^{2}\alpha+\alpha^{2}}{\alpha\cosh\alpha},
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
-2\mu p\,\frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}{\alpha\cosh\alpha}=S_{0}\; .
\end{equation*}
With $p=-\rho ge/2\mu$,

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e\,\frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}{\alpha\cosh\alpha}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. the viscously relaxed topography driven by the harmonic basal
traction $S_{0}\sin kx$ is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
       \frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\qquad \alpha=kh=\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda},
\end{equation*}
in the steady state $\dot e=0$.  Putting the restoring stress in the
form $\Delta\rho g e$ the conventional writing

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}=\chi\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
\end{equation*}
is obtained with

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
            \frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
          =\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}\,
            \frac{k h\cosh k h}{(k h)^{2}+\cosh^{2}k h}\;,
\end{equation*}
which for the notation $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ used in
$\sigma_{zz}=\rho g e\cos kx$ reduces to

\[
\chi(kh)=\dfrac{k h\cosh k h}{(k h)^{2}+\cosh^{2}k h}\;,
\]

the impermeable no-slip lid $B(0)=B'(0)=0$, $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$,
$\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}$ result.  In particular

\[
\chi(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),\qquad\alpha\ll1,
\]

so that

\[
e_{s}\simeq k h\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
      =\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda}\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad kh\ll1,
\]

the familiar thin–plate $e_{s}\propto kh\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ scaling.
For the order-unity wavenumber usually quoted, $\alpha\approx1$,

\[
\chi(1)=\frac{\cosh1}{1+\cosh^{2}1}\approx0.46,
\qquad e_{s}\approx0.46\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\]

i.e. the $O(1)$ dynamic-topography scaling $e_{s}\sim S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
with the geometrical factor $\alpha\cosh\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha]$.
The $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor is unity if the $\rho$ entering
$\sigma_{zz}=\rho ge$ is identified with the density contrast
$\Delta\rho$ that supports the topography.

The forms $\chi=kh/[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}]$ and the $1/2$ long-wave limit
are obtained if an additional free-slip condition $\sigma_{xz}(0)=0$
is imposed at the lid.  With the impermeable no-slip lid
$u_{x}(0)=u_{z}(0)=0$ used in the statement the shear stress at the lid
is not prescribed and the above $\alpha\cosh\alpha$ numerator is
retained.  This is the steady viscously relaxed topography quoted by
Colleague 1. ∎

1.text:
In the Stokes ansatz

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=A(z)\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=B(z)\cos kx ,\qquad p=P(z)\cos kx ,
\qquad B'=-kA ,
\end{equation*}
$\nabla^{4}B=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
P=\mu k^{-2}\!\left(B'''-k^{2}B'\right),\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\!\left(A'-kB\right)\sin kx
          =-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[B''+k^{2}B\Big]\sin kx ,
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=\!\left[-P+2\mu B'\right]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
so that with $D=d/dz$

\begin{equation*}
(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}B=0 .
\end{equation*}
Put the free surface at $z=0$ and the base at $z=h$ and
$\alpha=kh$.  The rigid lid

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0\Longleftrightarrow A(0)=0\Longleftrightarrow B'(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
and the steady kinematics $\dot e=u_{z}(0)=0$ give

\begin{equation*}
B(0)=0 .
\end{equation*}
Hence, up to two constants,

\begin{equation*}
B(z)=p\,z\cosh kz+\Bigl[-\frac{p}{k}+Dz\Bigr]\sinh kz,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
B'(z)=Dkz\cosh kz+\bigl[pkz+D\bigr]\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
and from $P=\mu k^{-2}(B'''-k^{2}B')$

\begin{equation*}
P(z)=2\mu\Bigl[\,p\cosh kz+D\sinh kz\Bigr],\qquad 
P(0)=2\mu p .
\end{equation*}
With $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho ge\cos kx$ and $B'(0)=0$

\begin{equation*}
-P(0)=\rho ge,\qquad P(0)=2\mu p\;\Longrightarrow\;
p=-\frac{\rho g}{2\mu}e ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
B'''(0)=2k^{2}p .
\end{equation*}
The stress free normal condition at the base,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\Longleftrightarrow P(h)=2\mu B'(h) ,
\end{equation*}
gives

\begin{equation*}
p\cosh\alpha+D\sinh\alpha=D\alpha\cosh\alpha+p\alpha\sinh\alpha
      +D\sinh\alpha,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
D=p\Bigl[\frac1\alpha-\tanh\alpha\Bigr],\qquad 
\alpha=kh .
\end{equation*}
The basal shear traction

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx
\Longleftrightarrow 
-\frac{\mu}{k}\Bigl[B''(h)+k^{2}B(h)\Bigr]=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
is with the $B$ above

\begin{equation*}
B''(h)+k^{2}B(h)=2k\Bigl[(D+p\alpha)\cosh\alpha+D\alpha\sinh\alpha\Bigr],
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
-2\mu\Bigl[(D+p\alpha)\cosh\alpha+D\alpha\sinh\alpha\Bigr]=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $D$ with $D=p[\alpha^{-1}-\tanh\alpha]$

\begin{equation*}
(D+p\alpha)\cosh\alpha+D\alpha\sinh\alpha
     =p\,\frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}{\alpha\cosh\alpha},
\end{equation*}
and hence

\begin{equation*}
-2\mu p\,\frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}{\alpha\cosh\alpha}=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
With $p=-\rho ge_{s}/2\mu$ the steady, $\dot e=0$, topography is

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}\,
\frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}{\alpha\cosh\alpha}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\qquad \alpha=kh=\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda},
\end{equation*}
the viscously relaxed dynamic topography driven by the harmonic basal
traction $S_{0}\sin kx$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq \alpha\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
      =\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda}\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},
\qquad \alpha\ll1 ,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}\xrightarrow[\alpha\to1]{} 
\frac{\cosh1}{1+\cosh^{2}1}\approx0.46,
\end{equation*}
the familiar $e_{s}\sim S_{0}/\rho g$ scaling with an $O(1)$ geometrical
factor.  Putting the restoring stress in the isostatic form
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$,
$e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, the conventionally quoted
geometrical factor is $f(\alpha)=\alpha\cosh\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+
\cosh^{2}\alpha]$ and

\begin{equation*}
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\Delta\rho}{\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
          =\frac{\Delta\rho}{\rho}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
which for the usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ in
$\sigma_{zz}=\rho ge$ is simply

\begin{equation*}
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\end{equation*}
$\chi\simeq kh$ for $kh\ll1$ and $\chi\to0$ for $kh\to0$, as required by
the $u_{x}=0$ side wall conditions $u_{x}=0$ at $x=0,\lambda/2$.  With
the notation $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ used in the statement this is the
$\chi(\alpha)=\alpha\cosh\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha]$ quoted by
Colleagues 1 and 3.  The compact form
$\chi=kh/[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}]$ is obtained if an additional
free–slip condition $\sigma_{xz}(0)=0$ is imposed at the rigid lid;
with the stated $u_{x}(0)=0$ only the $\cosh\alpha$ numerator is retained
and the sign $A'-kB$ in $\sigma_{xz}$ is the conventional one.  This is
the $O(1)$ viscous dynamic-topography factor $e_{s}\approx0.46\,
S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$ at $kh\sim1$ normally quoted in the literature. ∎

