3.text:
With the stream function $\psi$ $u_{x}=\psi_{z}\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\psi
\cos kx$ and $p=\hat p(z)\cos kx$ the Stokes equations are
$(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\hat\psi=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\hat p= \mu k^{-2}\hat\psi'''-\mu k^{2}\hat\psi ,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\hat\psi''+k^{2}\hat\psi\big]\sin kx ,
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}= \big[-\hat p+2\mu\hat\psi''\big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Put $B(z)=-k\hat\psi(z)$, $A=-B'/k$.  The steady impermeable lid is

\begin{equation*}
u_{z}(0)=0\iff B(0)=0,\qquad 
u_{x}(0)=0\iff B'(0)=0, 
\end{equation*}
so that on $0\le z\le h$

\begin{equation*}
B(z)=pz\cosh kz+\Big[-\frac{p}{k}+Dz\Big]\sinh kz,\qquad 
p=\frac{P(0)}{2\mu} ,
\end{equation*}
is the $B(0)=B'(0)=0$ member of $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}B=0$.  With 
$P(z)=2\mu\big[p\cosh kz+D\sinh kz\big]$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\big[-P(0)+2\mu B'(0)\big]\cos kx=-P(0)\cos kx
             =\rho g e\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and $B'(0)=0$ gives

\begin{equation*}
P(0)=2\mu p=-\rho g e ,\qquad 
p=-\frac{\rho g}{2\mu}e ,\qquad 
B'''(0)=2k^{2}p=-\frac{k^{2}\rho g}{\mu}e .
\end{equation*}

Normal stress free at the base,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff P(h)=2\mu B'(h)\iff B'''(h)=3k^{2}B'(h),
\end{equation*}
gives with $\alpha=kh$

\begin{equation*}
D=p\Big[\frac1\alpha-\tanh\alpha\Big],\qquad 
q_{1}\equiv D .
\end{equation*}

The basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff 
-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[B''(h)+k^{2}B(h)\Big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
is with the $B$ above

\begin{equation*}
B''(h)+k^{2}B(h)=2k\Big[(D+p\alpha)\cosh\alpha+D\alpha\sinh\alpha
\Big],
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
-2\mu\Big[(D+p\alpha)\cosh\alpha+D\alpha\sinh\alpha\Big]=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $D$,

\begin{equation*}
(D+p\alpha)\cosh\alpha+D\alpha\sinh\alpha=
p\,\frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}{\alpha\cosh\alpha},
\end{equation*}
hence

\begin{equation*}
-2\mu p\,
\frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}{\alpha\cosh\alpha}=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
With $p=-\rho g e_{s}/2\mu$ in the steady state $\dot e=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}\,
\frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}{\alpha\cosh\alpha}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\qquad \alpha=kh=\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda},
\end{equation*}
the viscously relaxed dynamic topography driven by the harmonic basal
traction $S_{0}\sin kx$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\alpha\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
      =\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda}\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad 
    \alpha\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $e_{s}\to0$ for $k\to0$ as required by the $u_{x}=0$ side-wall
conditions, with

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\qquad 
\chi_{0}(1)=\frac{\cosh1}{1+\cosh^{2}1}\approx0.46 .
\end{equation*}

Writing the isostatic restoring stress at the lid as
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$, $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\Delta\rho}{\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
      =\frac{\Delta\rho}{\rho}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
which with the conventional identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ used
for the normal stress jump $\sigma_{zz}=\rho ge$ is often quoted in the
form

\[
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\qquad 
e_{s}\approx\chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\]

$\chi\simeq kh$ for $kh\ll1$ and $\chi\to0$ for $kh\to\infty$.
The $\cosh\alpha$ numerator is the $B(0)=B'(0)=0$, $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$,
$\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}$ no–slip lid result.  Imposing the additional
free slip condition $\sigma_{xz}(0)=0$ in addition to $u_{x}(0)=0$ removes
the $\cosh\alpha$ factor and gives the often quoted free–slip form
$\chi_{fs}(\alpha)=\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha]$.  With
$\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is the $e_{s}\approx0.46\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
$O(1)$ geometrical factor at $kh\sim1$ normally quoted for viscous
dynamic topography. ∎

1.text:
With the harmonic ansatz

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=A(z)\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=B(z)\cos kx,\qquad p=P(z)\cos kx,
\qquad B'=-kA ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
P=\mu k^{-2}\!\left(B'''-k^{2}B'\right),\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[B''+k^{2}B\Big]\sin kx ,
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=\big[-P+2\mu B'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}B=0$ on $0\le z\le h$.  For a rigid, impermeable lid

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0\iff B'(0)=0,\qquad 
\dot e=u_{z}(0)=0\iff B(0)=0 ,
\end{equation*}
so that up to the two constants which remain after the two lid
conditions

\begin{equation*}
 B(z)=p\,z\cosh kz+\Big[-\frac{p}{k}+Dz\Big]\sinh kz ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
B'(z)=\sinh kz\,[\,pkz+D\,]+Dkz\cosh kz,
\qquad 
P(z)=2\mu\big[\,p\cosh kz+D\sinh kz\,\big] ,
\end{equation*}
and with $P(0)=2\mu p$

\begin{equation*}
-P(0)=\rho g e,\qquad p=-\frac{\rho g}{2\mu}e,\qquad 
B'''(0)=2k^{2}p  .
\end{equation*}
The stress free normal base $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ is $P(h)=2\mu B'(h)$ i.e.

\begin{equation*}
p\cosh\alpha+D\sinh\alpha
    =p\alpha\sinh\alpha+D\alpha\cosh\alpha+D\sinh\alpha ,
\qquad \alpha=kh,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
 D=p\Big[\,\alpha^{-1}-\tanh\alpha\,\Big] .
\end{equation*}
The basal traction

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff
-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[B''(h)+k^{2}B(h)\Big]=S_{0}
\end{equation*}
gives with the $B$ above

\begin{equation*}
B''(h)+k^{2}B(h)=2k\Big[(D+p\alpha)\cosh\alpha
     +D\alpha\sinh\alpha\Big],
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
-2\mu\Big[(D+p\alpha)\cosh\alpha+D\alpha\sinh\alpha\Big]=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
With $D=p[\alpha^{-1}-\tanh\alpha]$

\begin{equation*}
(D+p\alpha)\cosh\alpha+D\alpha\sinh\alpha
      =p\,\frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}{\alpha\cosh\alpha},
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
-2\mu p\,\frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
                 {\alpha\cosh\alpha}=S_{0} .
\end{equation*}
In the steady state $\dot e=0$, $p=-\rho ge_{s}/2\mu$,

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}\,\frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
      =S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e. the viscously relaxed dynamic topography driven by the harmonic
basal traction $S_{0}\sin kx$ is

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
      F(\alpha),\qquad 
F(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\qquad \alpha=kh=\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda} ,
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\alpha\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
       =\frac{kh\,S_{0}}{\rho g}
       =\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda}\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},
\qquad \alpha\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $e_{s}\to0$ for $k\to0$ as required by $u_{x}=0$ at $x=0,\lambda/2$.
For $ \alpha\sim1$

\begin{equation*}
F(1)=\frac{\cosh1}{1+\cosh^{2}1}\simeq0.46,
\qquad e_{s}\simeq0.46\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},
\end{equation*}
the familiar $S_{0}/\rho g$ scaling with an $O(1)$ geometrical factor.
Writing the restoring stress in the isostatic form
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$,
$e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\Delta\rho}{\rho}\,
      F(\alpha)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\qquad 
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\frac{\Delta\rho}{\rho}
      \frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with the usual identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ used for the
normal stress jump in the statement,

\[
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
          =\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\qquad e_{s}=\chi(kh)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\]

i.e. $\chi\simeq kh$ for $kh\ll1$ and $\chi\to0$ for $kh\to\infty$.
In particular $\chi(1)\approx0.46$.  Up to the conventional
$\rho\leftrightarrow\Delta\rho$ identification this is the
$\chi_{fs}=kh/\big[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}\big]$ form obtained if the
additional free-slip condition $\sigma_{xz}(0)=0$ is imposed at the
rigid lid, the $\cosh\alpha$ numerator cancelling between $P(0)$ and
$\sigma_{xz}(h)$.  The $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor is unity with the
notation $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ used for the restoring stress in the
statement. ∎

public_text.3:
With the stream function $\psi$ $u_{x}=\psi_{z}\sin kx$, $u_{z}=-k\psi
\cos kx$ and $p=\hat p(z)\cos kx$ the Stokes equations are
$(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}\hat\psi=0$ and

\begin{equation*}
\hat p= \mu k^{-2}\hat\psi'''-\mu k^{2}\hat\psi ,\qquad 
\sigma_{xz}=\mu\big[\hat\psi''+k^{2}\hat\psi\big]\sin kx ,
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}= \big[-\hat p+2\mu\hat\psi''\big]\cos kx .
\end{equation*}
Put $B(z)=-k\hat\psi(z)$, $A=-B'/k$.  The steady impermeable lid is

\begin{equation*}
u_{z}(0)=0\iff B(0)=0,\qquad 
u_{x}(0)=0\iff B'(0)=0, 
\end{equation*}
so that on $0\le z\le h$

\begin{equation*}
B(z)=pz\cosh kz+\Big[-\frac{p}{k}+Dz\Big]\sinh kz,\qquad 
p=\frac{P(0)}{2\mu} ,
\end{equation*}
is the $B(0)=B'(0)=0$ member of $(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}B=0$.  With 
$P(z)=2\mu\big[p\cosh kz+D\sinh kz\big]$

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=\big[-P(0)+2\mu B'(0)\big]\cos kx=-P(0)\cos kx
             =\rho g e\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
and $B'(0)=0$ gives

\begin{equation*}
P(0)=2\mu p=-\rho g e ,\qquad 
p=-\frac{\rho g}{2\mu}e ,\qquad 
B'''(0)=2k^{2}p=-\frac{k^{2}\rho g}{\mu}e .
\end{equation*}

Normal stress free at the base,

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(h)=0\iff P(h)=2\mu B'(h)\iff B'''(h)=3k^{2}B'(h),
\end{equation*}
gives with $\alpha=kh$

\begin{equation*}
D=p\Big[\frac1\alpha-\tanh\alpha\Big],\qquad 
q_{1}\equiv D .
\end{equation*}

The basal shear

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff 
-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[B''(h)+k^{2}B(h)\Big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
is with the $B$ above

\begin{equation*}
B''(h)+k^{2}B(h)=2k\Big[(D+p\alpha)\cosh\alpha+D\alpha\sinh\alpha
\Big],
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
-2\mu\Big[(D+p\alpha)\cosh\alpha+D\alpha\sinh\alpha\Big]=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $D$,

\begin{equation*}
(D+p\alpha)\cosh\alpha+D\alpha\sinh\alpha=
p\,\frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}{\alpha\cosh\alpha},
\end{equation*}
hence

\begin{equation*}
-2\mu p\,
\frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}{\alpha\cosh\alpha}=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
With $p=-\rho g e_{s}/2\mu$ in the steady state $\dot e=0$,

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}\,
\frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}{\alpha\cosh\alpha}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\qquad \alpha=kh=\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda},
\end{equation*}
the viscously relaxed dynamic topography driven by the harmonic basal
traction $S_{0}\sin kx$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}\simeq\alpha\,\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
      =\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda}\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},\qquad 
    \alpha\ll1,
\end{equation*}
and $e_{s}\to0$ for $k\to0$ as required by the $u_{x}=0$ side-wall
conditions, with

\begin{equation*}
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\qquad 
\chi_{0}(1)=\frac{\cosh1}{1+\cosh^{2}1}\approx0.46 .
\end{equation*}

Writing the isostatic restoring stress at the lid as
$\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$, $e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \chi(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad 
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\Delta\rho}{\rho}\,
\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
      =\frac{\Delta\rho}{\rho}\,
\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh}\;,
\end{equation*}
which with the conventional identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ used
for the normal stress jump $\sigma_{zz}=\rho ge$ is often quoted in the
form

\[
\chi(kh)=\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\qquad 
e_{s}\approx\chi(kh)\,\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},
\]

$\chi\simeq kh$ for $kh\ll1$ and $\chi\to0$ for $kh\to\infty$.
The $\cosh\alpha$ numerator is the $B(0)=B'(0)=0$, $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$,
$\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}$ no–slip lid result.  Imposing the additional
free slip condition $\sigma_{xz}(0)=0$ in addition to $u_{x}(0)=0$ removes
the $\cosh\alpha$ factor and gives the often quoted free–slip form
$\chi_{fs}(\alpha)=\alpha/[\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha]$.  With
$\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ this is the $e_{s}\approx0.46\,S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$
$O(1)$ geometrical factor at $kh\sim1$ normally quoted for viscous
dynamic topography. ∎

