$\chi_{fs}=kh/\big[\cosh^{2}kh+(kh)^{2}\big]$ form obtained if the
additional free-slip condition $\sigma_{xz}(0)=0$ is imposed at the
rigid lid, the $\cosh\alpha$ numerator cancelling between $P(0)$ and
$\sigma_{xz}(h)$.  The $\rho/\Delta\rho$ factor is unity with the
notation $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ used for the restoring stress in the
statement. ∎

1.text:
With the harmonic ansatz

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}=A(z)\sin kx,\qquad u_{z}=B(z)\cos kx,\qquad 
p=P(z)\cos kx,\qquad B'=-kA ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
P=\mu k^{-2}\big(B'''-k^{2}B'\big), \qquad 
\sigma_{xz}= -\frac{\mu}{k}\big[B''+k^{2}B\big]\sin kx ,
\qquad 
\sigma_{zz}=\big[-P+2\mu B'\big]\cos kx ,
\end{equation*}
$(D^{2}-k^{2})^{2}B=0$.  On the steady impermeable lid

\begin{equation*}
u_{x}(0)=0\iff B'(0)=0,\qquad \dot e=u_{z}(0)=0\iff B(0)=0,
\end{equation*}
so that on $0\le z\le h$

\begin{equation*}
B(z)=p\,z\cosh kz+\Big[-\frac{p}{k}+Dz\Big]\sinh kz,
\qquad 
P(z)=2\mu\big[p\cosh kz+D\sinh kz\big],
\end{equation*}
with $p=P(0)/2\mu$.  The lid normal stress is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{zz}(0)=-P(0)\cos kx=\rho g e\cos kx ,
\qquad P(0)=2\mu p=-\rho g e,
\qquad p=-\frac{\rho g}{2\mu}e,
\end{equation*}
hence $B'''(0)=2k^{2}p$.  The stress free base $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$ is
$P(h)=2\mu B'(h)$ i.e.

\begin{equation*}
p\cosh\alpha+D\sinh\alpha
      =p\alpha\sinh\alpha+D\alpha\cosh\alpha+D\sinh\alpha ,
\end{equation*}
$\alpha=kh$, whence

\begin{equation*}
D=p\Big[\alpha^{-1}-\tanh\alpha\Big],\qquad 
B'(h)=p\alpha\sinh\alpha+D\sinh\alpha+D\alpha\cosh\alpha .
\end{equation*}
The basal shear is

\begin{equation*}
\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}\sin kx\iff 
-\frac{\mu}{k}\Big[B''(h)+k^{2}B(h)\Big]=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
B''(h)+k^{2}B(h)=2k\Big[(D+p\alpha)\cosh\alpha
      +D\alpha\sinh\alpha\Big].
\end{equation*}
Eliminating $D$,

\begin{equation*}
(D+p\alpha)\cosh\alpha+D\alpha\sinh\alpha
      =p\,\frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}{\alpha\cosh\alpha},
\end{equation*}
so that

\begin{equation*}
-2\mu p\,\frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
                {\alpha\cosh\alpha}=S_{0}.
\end{equation*}
In the steady state $\dot e=0$, $p=-\rho g e_{s}/2\mu$,

\begin{equation*}
\rho g e_{s}\,\frac{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
                     {\alpha\cosh\alpha}=S_{0},
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}\,
      F(\alpha),\qquad 
F(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\qquad\alpha=kh=\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda},
\end{equation*}
is the viscously relaxed dynamic topography driven by the harmonic
basal traction $S_{0}\sin kx$.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
F(\alpha)=\alpha+O(\alpha^{3}),\qquad\alpha\ll1,
\qquad 
e_{s}\simeq\alpha\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
       =\frac{kh\,S_{0}}{\rho g}
       =\frac{2\pi h}{\lambda}\frac{S_{0}}{\rho g},
\end{equation*}
and $e_{s}\to0$ for $k\to0$ as required by $u_{x}\propto\sin kx=0$ at
$x=0,\lambda/2$.  With $F(1)=\cosh1/[\,1+\cosh^{2}1\,]\approx0.46$,
$e_{s}\approx0.46\,S_{0}/\rho g$ at $kh\sim1$.

Writing the isostatic restoring stress at the lid in the conventional
form $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\Delta\rho g e\cos kx$,
$e_{s}=\chi S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$,

\begin{equation*}
e_{s}= \frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}F(\alpha)
      \frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g}
      \frac{\Delta\rho}{\rho}\;,
\qquad 
\chi_{0}(\alpha)=\frac{\rho}{\Delta\rho}
       \frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with the identification $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$ used for the normal
stress jump in the statement

\[
e_{s}= \chi(\alpha)\frac{S_{0}}{\Delta\rho g},\qquad
\chi(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha\cosh\alpha}
                 {\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
          =\frac{kh\cosh kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\]
$\chi\simeq kh$ for $kh\ll1$, $\chi\to0$ for $kh\to\infty$ and
$\chi(1)\approx0.46$.  This is the $B(0)=B'(0)=0$, $\sigma_{zz}(h)=0$,
$\sigma_{xz}(h)=S_{0}$ no-slip lid result quoted above.

If the physically usual free slip condition $\sigma_{xz}(0)=0$ is
additionally imposed at the rigid lid, $E=-kD$ in the stream function
form, the pressure $P(0)$ and the basal shear share the same
$\cosh\alpha$ factor and it cancels.  The familiar free-slip form is
then

\[
e_{s}^{fs}= \frac{S_{0}}{\rho g}
            \frac{\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha},
\qquad 
\chi_{fs}(\alpha)=\frac{\alpha}{\alpha^{2}+\cosh^{2}\alpha}
        =\frac{kh}{(kh)^{2}+\cosh^{2}kh},
\]
so that with the conventional $\rho\equiv\Delta\rho$
$e_{s}^{fs}=\chi_{fs}S_{0}/\Delta\rho g$, $\chi_{fs}\simeq\alpha$
for $\alpha\ll1$ and $\chi_{fs}(1)\approx0.30$.  The $\cosh\alpha$
numerator retained above is the one obtained with the top conditions
as stated, $u_{x}(0)=0$, $\sigma_{zz}(0)=\rho g e$, $\,B(0)=B'(0)=0$,
with no extra $\sigma_{xz}(0)=0$ imposed.  Up to the conventional
$\rho\leftrightarrow\Delta\rho$ identification this is the $O(1)$
geometrical factor normally quoted for viscous dynamic topography. ∎

